<component name="InspectionProjectProfileManager">
  <profile version="1.0">
    <option name="myName" value="Project Default" />

    <!--
      Five inspections are disabled. Each is something the repository cannot
      fix in source, with the evidence recorded. Everything the IDE reported
      that COULD be fixed was fixed instead of suppressed:

        unused imports        the star-import shims became explicit
                              `from X import Y as Y` re-exports
        no generic font name  every use site now names its generic fallback
        protected member      jaccard, normalize_title, detect_contradictions
                              and build_error_payload were made public, since
                              other modules and the tests already used them
        unused local          the optional-import fallbacks `del` their
                              parameters, keeping the signature contract
        unused CSS symbol     four genuinely dead rules (.hist-box, .hist-label,
                              .bt-hit, .bt-miss) were deleted
        incorrect type        locals read out of untyped dicts are annotated,
                              so the `is not None` guards actually narrow

      On "incorrect type" specifically: the root cause was dict.get() returning
      Any | None, which gave the existing guards nothing to narrow and made
      every downstream use look wrong. The fix is annotations at the point of
      read (chg_1d: float | None = metrics.get(...)), which are erased at
      runtime — no behaviour change, the guards simply became checkable. That
      inspection is therefore back ON rather than suppressed.

      What remains, and why source cannot fix it:

      CssUnusedSymbol — the 37 remaining selectors are all emitted at runtime.
        Some come from Streamlit's own DOM (.stApp, .stAlert), the rest from
        class names built in Python f-strings; _SIGNAL_CLASS_MAP in
        components/snapshot.py is the clearest example. Each was checked
        against the Python sources before disabling; the four that turned out
        to be genuinely dead were removed rather than hidden.

      UndefinedParamsPresent — flags `python-version` and `cache` on
        actions/setup-python@v7 in .github/workflows/ci.yml. Both are valid;
        the bundled GitHub Actions schema is behind the action version. There
        is nothing to change in the workflow.

      PyPackageRequirements — reads requirements.txt only. The dev toolchain is
        declared in pyproject.toml under [project.optional-dependencies] and
        pinned in requirements-dev.txt, so `import pytest` in tests/ is
        properly declared; this inspection just cannot see either file.

      GrazieStyle — checks prose against American English. The project writes
        British English on purpose, and some of it is public API:
        analyse_asset, analyse_market_context, tests/test_optimisation.py.
        All 35 findings were British spellings such as "analyse" and
        "behaviour". Real grammar errors still come from GrazieInspection,
        which stays ON.

      OutdatedRequirementInspection — flags every pin in requirements*.txt
        that has a newer release. The pins are deliberate (CI reproduces
        them), and Dependabot opens one PR per upgrade, so each bump gets its
        own CI run. Some upgrades need code work first: pandas 3 conflicts
        with the pandas-stubs<3 pin in pyproject.toml.
    -->
    <inspection_tool class="CssUnusedSymbol" enabled="false" level="WARNING" enabled_by_default="false" />
    <inspection_tool class="UndefinedParamsPresent" enabled="false" level="WARNING" enabled_by_default="false" />
    <inspection_tool class="PyPackageRequirementsInspection" enabled="false" level="WEAK WARNING" enabled_by_default="false" />
    <inspection_tool class="GrazieStyle" enabled="false" level="TYPO" enabled_by_default="false" />
    <inspection_tool class="OutdatedRequirementInspection" enabled="false" level="WARNING" enabled_by_default="false" />
  </profile>
</component>
